          <li><strong>The exact hue of the zenith</strong> depends on the observer's spectral response — color is a perceptual construction, not a spectral property. The CIE-1931 chromaticity of natural zenith skylight sits around (0.17, 0.21), well outside the gamut of most displays.</li>
          <li><strong>Exoplanet context:</strong> a Rayleigh-scattering atmosphere on a planet orbiting an M-dwarf would appear distinctly different, since the incident spectrum is red-weighted. Sky color is a <em>joint</em> function of illuminant spectrum, scatterer regime, and absorber column — not a property of the atmosphere alone.</li>
